---
// ForkKnife's front door on production, while the app itself is still being built on `forknifedev`.
//
// Condensed from docs/thesis.md, for the same reason as FortKnight's: the URL should say what the face
// argues before the tool behind it is finished. The real menu grid lives on the face branch and replaces
// this file when it merges.
import FaceLayout from "../../layouts/FaceLayout.astro";
import { facePath } from "../../lib/faces.js";

const fortknight = facePath("fortknight");
---
<FaceLayout title="ForkKnife" face="forkknife">
  <section class="panel">
    <h1>ForkKnife</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Feeding yourself is a scheduling problem wearing a recipe problem’s clothes.
      Plan the cooking, not the eating.
    </p>

    <h2>What it argues</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Nobody’s actual problem is inspiration.</strong> Recipe apps solve discovery. The question
        at six in the evening is what is already made, and what tonight costs you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Leftovers are the load-bearing structure.</strong> About eight dishes cover fourteen days
        because six are eaten twice — never the next day, and at most three days later. That constraint is
        the design.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Leftovers cross meals, one direction only.</strong> Sunday’s dinner is Tuesday’s breakfast;
        a morning meal’s leftovers never move to a later one. Food does not respect the slots we file it
        under.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A menu is a bill of work.</strong> Choosing a dish is choosing prep and cooking minutes on
        specific days, so the menu does not stay in the food app — its tasks land in the
        same profile <a href={fortknight}>FortKnight</a> plans from.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>That is why it is a face, not a product.</strong> Two faces, one on-device profile. A
        standalone meal planner would have to lie about what dinner costs the rest of your week.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What is here now</h2>
    <p>
      The menu planner is still being built and is not on the live site yet.
    </p>
  </section>
</FaceLayout>
